//! The Window module: a window and a 2D drawing surface, over SDL2's C API.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.5's first two items, on the foundation ADR-0163 chose after §8.5's own
//! foundation turned out to be unreachable.
//!
//! # Why SDL2 and not Cocoa
//!
//! §2.1 imagined "Cocoa via `#foreign`", and every Cocoa call goes through `objc_msgSend`, which is
//! **variadic** — and a variadic call is E0289 here because Cranelift's `Signature` has no notion of a
//! variadic boundary (ADR-0162 §2). That blocker is upstream, so it is not a matter of doing more work in
//! this project.
//!
//! SDL2 needs none of it: every routine below is a plain C function taking scalars and pointers. It also
//! works on Linux, which §0's decision #6 makes a target and which Cocoa never was. The cost is that a
//! drawing program needs SDL2 installed and that this module binds somebody else's library rather than the
//! platform — stated rather than glossed, and argued at length in ADR-0163 §1.
//!
//! # Using it
//!
//! SDL2 is not in the C driver's default search path, so a build needs to be told where it is:
//!
//! ```text
//! jr build my_program.jr -L /opt/homebrew/lib
//! ```
//!
//! or `JR_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/homebrew/lib`. That is a property of the machine compiling and deliberately not
//! something a source file can say (ADR-0163 §2).
//!
//! **This module does not run under `jr run`.** The comptime VM resolves foreign symbols from the compiler's
//! own process image rather than from a link line (ADR-0158 §3's mechanism), so it can reach libc and nothing
//! else. A program that draws is a `jr build` program.
//!
//! # Why the handles are `*u8` and not opaque types
//!
//! `SDL_Window` and `SDL_Renderer` are opaque in C — a caller only ever holds a pointer. A Jairs
//! `Window :: struct { handle: *u8; }` would add a name and one field, and the name is worth having, so that
//! is what the two wrappers below are. The *raw* bindings take `*u8` because that is what an opaque pointer
//! is, and because a `#foreign` signature naming a Jairs struct it never dereferences would be describing a
//! layout SDL2 does not have.
//!
//! # The event loop, and why it needed no compiler change (ADR-0165)
//!
//! ADR-0164 §5 claimed this module could not have an event loop, because `SDL_Event` is a **union** and E0286
//! refuses a union at a `#foreign` boundary. **That was wrong, and it was wrong for not probing**: E0286
//! refuses an aggregate crossing **by value**, and `SDL_PollEvent` takes a `SDL_Event *`. A pointer to a union
//! is just a pointer, exactly as a `*Rect` is.
//!
//! So the loop below needed no compiler work at all. `Event` is a `#place` overlay (ADR-0144) whose every
//! offset is checked against C's `offsetof` — and `#place` is precisely the union mechanism, since two fields
//! at one offset is what a union *is*. `key_sym` and `mouse_x` genuinely share offset 20, and that overlap is
//! the union showing through rather than a mistake.
//!
//! **What is actually guaranteed, stated exactly.** SDL's own header says of `type`: *"Event type, shared with
//! all events"* — so offset 0 is contractual. The rest are SDL2 ABI offsets, stable across the 2.x series by
//! the same promise that makes `SDL_Event` a fixed 56 bytes, and `LAYOUT_IS_SDL2` below fails the build if
//! this platform disagrees about the size. That is the honest line: offset 0 is a documented invariant, the
//! others are an ABI this module asserts and checks rather than assumes.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No texture or image loading.** `SDL_image` is a second library, and decoding in Jairs wants `File`
//! (which exists) plus a decoder, which is its own item in §8.5.
//!
//! **No GPU layer.** SDL2's renderer is a 2D API and covers §8.5's first two items. Metal is Objective-C and
//! inherits §1's refusal; Vulkan is a C API and would work the way this does. The choice waits for whichever
//! item needs a shader (ADR-0163 §4).

/// `SDL_INIT_VIDEO`, the only subsystem this module needs.
///
/// 0x20. The flags are a bitmask and stable across SDL2's lifetime, unlike the `O_*` constants `File` had to
/// hedge about (ADR-0157 §1): SDL defines them itself rather than inheriting a platform's.
INIT_VIDEO :: 32;

/// `SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED`, for a window with no preferred corner.
///
/// 0x2FFF0000. A magic number in the header too — SDL computes it as `SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED_MASK | 0`, and
/// the mask is what this is.
CENTERED :: 805240832;

/// `SDL_WINDOW_SHOWN` — the window is visible immediately.
SHOWN :: 4;

/// `SDL_WINDOW_HIDDEN` — created but not mapped.
///
/// What a test wants: it exercises the whole creation path without requiring a display, which is why the
/// probe behind ADR-0163 §1 used it.
HIDDEN :: 8;

/// `SDL_WINDOW_RESIZABLE`.
RESIZABLE :: 32;

/// `SDL_RENDERER_ACCELERATED` — prefer a GPU-backed renderer.
ACCELERATED :: 2;

/// `SDL_RENDERER_SOFTWARE` — force the software path, which any machine has.
SOFTWARE :: 1;

/// `SDL_RENDERER_PRESENTVSYNC`.
PRESENT_VSYNC :: 4;

/// `SDL_QUIT` — the application was asked to stop.
QUIT :: 256;

/// `SDL_WINDOWEVENT` — something happened to a window; `window_event` says what.
WINDOW_EVENT :: 512;

/// `SDL_WINDOWEVENT_CLOSE`, the `window_event` value for a close box (or Cmd-W).
///
/// Small because it is an enumerator inside the window-event kind, not an event type of its own.
WINDOW_CLOSE :: 14;

/// `SDL_KEYDOWN`.
KEY_DOWN :: 768;

/// `SDL_KEYUP`.
KEY_UP :: 769;

/// `SDL_MOUSEMOTION`.
MOUSE_MOTION :: 1024;

/// `SDL_MOUSEBUTTONDOWN`.
MOUSE_DOWN :: 1025;

/// `SDL_MOUSEBUTTONUP`.
MOUSE_UP :: 1026;

/// `SDLK_ESCAPE`, the one keycode worth naming here.
///
/// 27 — a keycode below 128 is its ASCII value, which is why this is the *only* one that needs a constant: a
/// caller comparing against `'a'` can do so directly, and a caller who needs F-keys wants a table this module
/// deliberately does not carry.
KEY_ESCAPE :: 27;

/// An `SDL_Event`, as a `#place` overlay of the union's members (ADR-0165 §2).
///
/// **Fields share offsets on purpose.** `key_sym` and `mouse_x` are both at 20, `key_state`, `mouse_button`
/// and `window_event` at 12 and 16 — that is what a union is, and `#place` (ADR-0144) is the mechanism for
/// saying so. Which field is meaningful is decided by `kind`, exactly as in C.
///
/// **56 bytes**, which `LAYOUT_IS_SDL2` asserts: SDL writes the whole union through the pointer, so a smaller
/// struct is a stack overwrite and a larger one wastes nothing but lies about the ABI.
Event :: struct {
    /// The event type — `QUIT`, `KEY_DOWN`, `MOUSE_MOTION` and so on.
    ///
    /// **Offset 0 is contractual**, not inferred: SDL's header documents it as "shared with all events".
    kind: u32 #place 0;
    /// Milliseconds since SDL started, as `SDL_GetTicks` reports them.
    timestamp: u32 #place 4;
    /// Which window the event is about, for the kinds that concern one.
    window_id: u32 #place 8;
    /// For `WINDOW_EVENT`: which window thing happened — compare against `WINDOW_CLOSE`.
    window_event: u8 #place 12;
    /// For `KEY_DOWN`/`KEY_UP`: 1 while the key is held.
    key_state: u8 #place 12;
    /// For a key event: non-zero when this is an auto-repeat rather than a fresh press.
    key_repeat: u8 #place 13;
    /// For `MOUSE_DOWN`/`MOUSE_UP`: which button, 1 for left.
    mouse_button: u8 #place 16;
    /// For a key event: the keycode. Below 128 it is the ASCII value.
    key_sym: u32 #place 20;
    /// For a mouse event: x, in pixels from the window's left. Shares offset 20 with `key_sym`.
    mouse_x: s32 #place 20;
    /// For a mouse event: y, in pixels from the top.
    mouse_y: s32 #place 24;
    /// The rest of the union, so the struct is the 56 bytes SDL writes.
    ///
    /// Never read. It exists so that `sdl_poll` cannot write past this struct — the difference between a
    /// working poll and a stack overwrite that appears as a corrupted unrelated local.
    tail: [28]u8 #place 28;
}

/// Whether `Event` is the 56 bytes SDL2 writes through the pointer.
///
/// **A file-scope constant at last** (ADR-0180 §3). It was a *procedure*, and the comment here recorded
/// why: `size_of` of a struct was unreachable from a file-scope constant — E0230, "a name failed to
/// resolve at file scope" — because the signature phase folded the call and then threw the value away,
/// so `jr-mir`'s thunk found no value and resolved `size_of` as a name. `Socket` had moved the same
/// check into a procedure for the same reason (ADR-0158 §4).
///
/// An overlay is a claim about somebody else's ABI, so the claim is checkable. A caller who wants the
/// check to be fatal reads it and exits; a caller who does not can ignore it.
LAYOUT_IS_SDL2 :: size_of(Event) == 56;

/// An `SDL_Rect`: four C `int`s.
///
/// `s32` fields, matching C's `int` on both supported targets, and **not** `s64`: SDL reads sixteen bytes
/// through the pointer, so a wider field would make the second rectangle's worth of data land in the first's
/// height. Passed by pointer, as SDL's own API does, so ADR-0161's aggregate-by-value work is not needed here
/// — which is part of why SDL2 was reachable before Cocoa was.
Rect :: struct {
    /// Left edge, in pixels from the drawing area's left.
    x: s32;
    /// Top edge, in pixels from the top.
    y: s32;
    /// Width in pixels.
    w: s32;
    /// Height in pixels.
    h: s32;
}

/// A window.
///
/// A named one-field struct rather than a bare pointer, for the reason `File` wraps a descriptor (ADR-0157
/// §1): a handle and a count are both machine words, and the compiler will not confuse them once one has a
/// name.
Window :: struct {
    /// The `SDL_Window *`. Null when the window is not open.
    handle: *u8;
}

/// A 2D renderer bound to a window.
Renderer :: struct {
    /// The `SDL_Renderer *`. Null when the renderer is not created.
    handle: *u8;
}

/// Starts the video subsystem.
///
/// `#must`: nothing else here works if this failed, and a caller who ignores the flag gets a null window and
/// a null renderer and no idea why — the exact chain of one-level-removed failures the marker exists for
/// (ADR-0151).
///
/// Idempotent in SDL, so calling it twice is defined and cheap.
start :: () -> bool #must {
    return sdl_init(INIT_VIDEO) == 0;
}

/// Shuts the video subsystem down. Not `#must`: there is nothing to recover from and nothing to report.
stop :: () {
    sdl_quit();
}

/// Opens a window `width` by `height`, centred, with `flags`.
///
/// `title` must be NUL-terminated, which is why it is a `*u8` and not a `string`: SDL reads to a NUL and a
/// Jairs `string` carries a count instead. `File.nul_terminated`'s trick — a scratch copy from temporary
/// storage — is the way to pass a computed title, and it is deliberately not done here: a window title
/// usually outlives the call, and temporary storage does not.
///
/// `#must`, and the flag is the honest answer rather than a null check: SDL fails to create a window for
/// reasons a caller can act on — no display, an unsupported flag combination — and a null handle read as
/// "not yet" instead of "never" is how a draw loop spins forever.
open :: (title: *u8, width: s64, height: s64, flags: s64) -> (Window, bool) #must {
    w: Window;
    w.handle = sdl_create_window(title, CENTERED, CENTERED, width, height, flags);
    return w, w.handle != null;
}

/// Closes `window` and marks it closed.
///
/// Safe on an already-closed window, so a caller can close on every path without tracking whether they got
/// one — the property that makes `File.close` usable where a `defer` would go (ADR-0157 §3).
close :: (window: *Window) {
    if window.handle != null {
        sdl_destroy_window(window.handle);
    }
    window.handle = null;
}

/// Creates a renderer for `window`.
///
/// `SOFTWARE` rather than `ACCELERATED` as the flag a caller should reach for first, and the docs say so
/// because the default is surprising: `ACCELERATED` *fails* on a machine with no GPU driver, where software
/// always works. A caller who wants acceleration should ask for it and check the flag.
///
/// The `-1` index means "the first driver that satisfies the flags", which is what every caller wants and
/// what a named constant would only obscure.
renderer_for :: (window: *Window, flags: s64) -> (Renderer, bool) #must {
    r: Renderer;
    if window.handle == null {
        r.handle = null;
        return r, false;
    }
    r.handle = sdl_create_renderer(window.handle, -1, flags);
    return r, r.handle != null;
}

/// Destroys `renderer`. Safe on an already-destroyed one.
destroy :: (renderer: *Renderer) {
    if renderer.handle != null {
        sdl_destroy_renderer(renderer.handle);
    }
    renderer.handle = null;
}

/// Sets the colour subsequent drawing uses, as four 0-255 channels.
///
/// `#must` like every other call that can fail, and it genuinely can: a destroyed renderer returns an error
/// rather than trapping, and a caller who ignores it draws in whatever colour was set before.
set_color :: (renderer: *Renderer, red: s64, green: s64, blue: s64, alpha: s64) -> bool #must {
    if renderer.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_set_draw_color(renderer.handle, red, green, blue, alpha) == 0;
}

/// Fills the whole drawing area with the current colour.
clear :: (renderer: *Renderer) -> bool #must {
    if renderer.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_render_clear(renderer.handle) == 0;
}

/// Fills `rect` with the current colour.
fill :: (renderer: *Renderer, rect: *Rect) -> bool #must {
    if renderer.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_fill_rect(renderer.handle, rect) == 0;
}

/// Strokes `rect`'s outline with the current colour.
outline :: (renderer: *Renderer, rect: *Rect) -> bool #must {
    if renderer.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_draw_rect(renderer.handle, rect) == 0;
}

/// Draws a line from `(x1, y1)` to `(x2, y2)`.
line :: (renderer: *Renderer, x1: s64, y1: s64, x2: s64, y2: s64) -> bool #must {
    if renderer.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_draw_line(renderer.handle, x1, y1, x2, y2) == 0;
}

/// Shows everything drawn since the last present.
///
/// Not `#must`: SDL's own `SDL_RenderPresent` returns nothing, so there is no failure to report and inventing
/// one would be a lie about what the library says.
present :: (renderer: *Renderer) {
    if renderer.handle != null {
        sdl_render_present(renderer.handle);
    }
}

/// Sleeps for `milliseconds`.
///
/// Here rather than in `Time` because `Time` deliberately has no sleep (ADR-0155 §1: a blocking call in the
/// comptime VM would mean compilation that pauses) — and this module already cannot run in the VM at all, so
/// the objection does not apply. A drawing program needs to yield the CPU between frames, and this is the
/// only way it can.
delay :: (milliseconds: s64) {
    sdl_delay(milliseconds);
}

/// A rectangle, built from four `s64`s a caller already has.
///
/// A constructor because the four `cast(s32, …)`es are noise at every call site, and because narrowing is
/// exactly where a caller would forget one and write a `s64` into a field SDL reads as two.
rect :: (x: s64, y: s64, w: s64, h: s64) -> Rect {
    r: Rect;
    r.x = cast(s32, x);
    r.y = cast(s32, y);
    r.w = cast(s32, w);
    r.h = cast(s32, h);
    return r;
}

/// How many video drivers SDL was built with.
///
/// Not useful for drawing, and useful for a *test*: it is the cheapest call that proves the library linked
/// and ran, with no display and no window. It is what ADR-0163 §1's first probe used.
video_driver_count :: () -> s64 {
    return sdl_video_drivers();
}

/// Takes the next event, if one is waiting.
///
/// The `bool` is "an event was written", **not** a failure flag, so this is deliberately not `#must`: an empty
/// queue is the normal case in a draw loop and `#must` on it would make every frame carry a ceremony for a
/// non-event. That is the line ADR-0151 draws — the marker is for a failure a caller would otherwise miss, and
/// "nothing happened yet" is not one.
///
/// ```text
/// while running {
///     e: Event;
///     while next_event(*e) {
///         if should_close(*e) { running = false; }
///     }
///     // draw
/// }
/// ```
next_event :: (event: *Event) -> bool {
    return sdl_poll_event(event) == 1;
}

/// Moves pending device state into the queue.
///
/// `next_event` pumps implicitly, so a caller who only polls never needs this. It is here for a caller who
/// wants the queue filled at a known point — before a timing measurement, say — rather than as a side effect.
pump :: () {
    sdl_pump_events();
}

/// Puts `event` on the queue, so it comes back from `next_event`.
///
/// `#must`: SDL refuses a push when the queue is full or an event filter drops it, and a caller who ignores
/// that waits forever for something that was never enqueued.
///
/// Its first use is a **test**: pushing a synthetic `QUIT` and polling it back exercises the whole overlay
/// with no window manager and no human, which is how ADR-0165 §1 established the loop works at all.
push :: (event: *Event) -> bool #must {
    return sdl_push_event(event) == 1;
}

/// Whether `event` means the program should stop.
///
/// True for `QUIT` — the Dock's Quit, Cmd-Q, a signal — **and** for a window-close event, because a user
/// clicking the close box of the only window means the same thing and a caller who checked only `QUIT` would
/// have a window that refuses to shut. Handling both is what almost every program wants, and a caller who
/// wants to distinguish them still can, by reading `kind` directly.
should_close :: (event: *Event) -> bool {
    // The fields are widened rather than the constants narrowed, and every comparison in this module does the
    // same. A `#place` overlay's fields are C widths — `u32`, `u8` — while a Jairs constant is `s64` and there
    // is no syntax to annotate one (probed: `QUIT : u32 : 256` does not parse). Widening a `u32` to `s64` is
    // lossless in every case, so this direction cannot be wrong; narrowing the constant could be.
    if cast(s64, event.kind) == QUIT {
        return true;
    }
    return cast(s64, event.kind) == WINDOW_EVENT && cast(s64, event.window_event) == WINDOW_CLOSE;
}

/// Whether `event` is a fresh press of `keycode` — not an auto-repeat.
///
/// `key_repeat` is checked, because a held key produces a stream of `KEY_DOWN`s and a caller asking "was this
/// pressed" almost never wants forty of them. A caller who *does* want the repeats reads `kind` and `key_sym`
/// directly.
pressed :: (event: *Event, keycode: s64) -> bool {
    // `keycode` is `s64` so a caller can pass `KEY_ESCAPE` or a character literal without a cast, which is the
    // whole point of a helper: the widening happens once here rather than at every call site.
    return cast(s64, event.kind) == KEY_DOWN && cast(s64, event.key_sym) == keycode && event.key_repeat == 0;
}

/// A synthetic `QUIT` event, ready to `push`.
///
/// A constructor because a caller building one by hand has to know that `kind` is the only field that matters
/// and that the rest must be zero — and because the *test* for this module needs one.
quit_event :: () -> Event {
    e: Event;
    e.kind = cast(u32, QUIT);
    return e;
}

/// Drains the queue and reports whether anything in it asked the program to stop.
///
/// **The routine almost every program wants**, and the one a caller writes wrong first. SDL's queue does not
/// promise one-push-one-poll: a single `next_event` can return nothing while an event is pending, because
/// `SDL_PollEvent` pumps the device state as a side effect and the pump can deliver on a later call. A caller
/// who polls once per frame and acts on the result will miss events; a caller who drains will not. That was
/// found by writing it — a test that polled exactly once per push failed on the *second* push and passed on
/// the first, which is the shape of a bug nobody debugs correctly at three in the morning.
///
/// It drains **everything** rather than stopping at the first close, so the queue is empty when it returns and
/// a caller's next frame starts clean. `limit` bounds the work, because an event source that produces faster
/// than this drains would otherwise hang the frame — mouse motion during a drag genuinely can.
///
/// Not `#must`: `false` is the ordinary answer on almost every frame.
wants_to_close :: (limit: s64) -> bool {
    closing := false;
    e: Event;
    i := 0;
    while i < limit {
        if !next_event(*e) {
            return closing;
        }
        if should_close(*e) {
            closing = true;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return closing;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Private: the raw SDL2 bindings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Everything below is **not exported** (ADR-0054 §2, ADR-0179 §6). These are the raw C symbols and
// the library handle; a caller wants `open` and `fill`, not `sdl_create_window`. They used to be
// exported into every importer's flat namespace, seventeen names deep, because this file had no
// `#scope_module` — which `modules/Basic`, `File`, `File_Utilities` and `List` all have.
#scope_module

/// The SDL2 shared library.
///
/// Named `"SDL2"`, which becomes `-lSDL2`. Where it lives is the operator's business — see the module docs.
sdl :: #system_library "SDL2";

/// libc `SDL_Init`.
sdl_init :: (flags: s64) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_Init";

/// libc `SDL_Quit`.
sdl_quit :: () #foreign sdl "SDL_Quit";

/// `SDL_CreateWindow(const char *title, int x, int y, int w, int h, Uint32 flags)`.
sdl_create_window :: (title: *u8, x: s64, y: s64, w: s64, h: s64, flags: s64) -> *u8 #foreign sdl "SDL_CreateWindow";

/// `SDL_DestroyWindow`.
sdl_destroy_window :: (window: *u8) #foreign sdl "SDL_DestroyWindow";

/// `SDL_CreateRenderer(SDL_Window *, int index, Uint32 flags)`.
sdl_create_renderer :: (window: *u8, index: s64, flags: s64) -> *u8 #foreign sdl "SDL_CreateRenderer";

/// `SDL_DestroyRenderer`.
sdl_destroy_renderer :: (renderer: *u8) #foreign sdl "SDL_DestroyRenderer";

/// `SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(SDL_Renderer *, Uint8 r, Uint8 g, Uint8 b, Uint8 a)`.
sdl_set_draw_color :: (renderer: *u8, red: s64, green: s64, blue: s64, alpha: s64) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_SetRenderDrawColor";

/// `SDL_RenderClear`.
sdl_render_clear :: (renderer: *u8) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_RenderClear";

/// `SDL_RenderFillRect(SDL_Renderer *, const SDL_Rect *)`.
sdl_fill_rect :: (renderer: *u8, rect: *Rect) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_RenderFillRect";

/// `SDL_RenderDrawRect`.
sdl_draw_rect :: (renderer: *u8, rect: *Rect) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_RenderDrawRect";

/// `SDL_RenderDrawLine`.
sdl_draw_line :: (renderer: *u8, x1: s64, y1: s64, x2: s64, y2: s64) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_RenderDrawLine";

/// `SDL_RenderPresent`.
sdl_render_present :: (renderer: *u8) #foreign sdl "SDL_RenderPresent";

/// `SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers`.
sdl_video_drivers :: () -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers";

/// `SDL_Delay(Uint32 ms)`.
sdl_delay :: (milliseconds: s64) #foreign sdl "SDL_Delay";

/// `SDL_PollEvent(SDL_Event *)` — 1 when an event was written, 0 when the queue is empty.
///
/// Takes a **pointer**, which is the whole reason ADR-0164 §5's refusal did not apply (ADR-0165 §1).
sdl_poll_event :: (event: *Event) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_PollEvent";

/// `SDL_PushEvent(SDL_Event *)` — 1 on success.
sdl_push_event :: (event: *Event) -> s64 #foreign sdl "SDL_PushEvent";

/// `SDL_PumpEvents` — moves pending device state into the queue.
sdl_pump_events :: () #foreign sdl "SDL_PumpEvents";
